OpenCrypto scatter/gather SHA-256 qualification module
====================================================

This explicit test module exercises the actual kernel crypto interface. It is
not a verifier backend, default build target or boot dependency. An optional
bounded component timing mode is described below.
Do not install/load it on the build host. Run it only in a disposable test VM
with a matching kernel after VM execution is authorized. Use an INVARIANTS
kernel so request-contract checks are active. A hung request must fail the VM
runner deadline; the module cannot abandon memory still owned by a provider.

Compile only with the configured kernel flags:
  make -C /usr/src/tools/test/construction -j16 verity-crypto-kernel-compile \
    VERITY_KERNEL_OBJDIR=/usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC

For the explicit module artifact, supply the matching kernel object directory:
  make -C /usr/src/tools/test/construction/verity_crypto \
    MAKEOBJDIRPREFIX=/usr/obj SYSDIR=/usr/src/sys \
    KERNBUILDDIR=/usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC obj
  make -C /usr/src/tools/test/construction/verity_crypto -j16 \
    MAKEOBJDIRPREFIX=/usr/obj SYSDIR=/usr/src/sys \
    KERNBUILDDIR=/usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC WERROR=-Werror all

In the disposable VM only, choose an installed provider instance using
  kenv debug.verity_crypto_test.provider=cryptosoft0
then kldload the staged matching verity_crypto_test.ko by its explicit path.
Require successful load AND the console result:
  verity_crypto_test: provider=cryptosoft0 passed=64 expected=64 error=0
Unload the test module before repeating with aesni0 or another applicable
provider. The test defaults to cryptosoft0; a missing requested provider fails,
and a prefix resolving to a different instance fails. Missing acceleration is
an unavailable matrix row, not evidence that an accelerated provider passed.

The 64 cases comprise SHA256("abc") plus seven salt lengths, three block
lengths and three payload splits. Each uses one UIO payload for salt and data,
with a separate iovec for digest storage. No AAD or concatenation allocation is
used. Scalar SHA-256 computes the reference; the abc vector also checks a fixed
known digest. Cases check digest equality, unchanged input hash and digest
boundary sentinels. Salt lengths include zero, SHA-256 block boundaries and the
verity format maximum. All requests share one session and are fully completed
before freeing request, session or buffer storage. Dispatch/provider errors,
including migration/EAGAIN, fail rather than count as passed.

Compile/link success is not runtime qualification. Still required: execute the
matrix, negative-control validation, provider migration/error injection,
concurrent request/lifetime tests, and measured dispatch/FPU overhead before
integrating an accelerated verifier backend. The test does not prove absence
of provider-internal copies, bounded provider queues, performance or disk I/O.

Optional cost comparison (VM only)
----------------------------------
Before loading the module, explicitly set:
  kenv debug.verity_crypto_test.iterations=2000
The default is zero (correctness matrix only); negative values or values above
10000 reject the run before session allocation. After all 64 correctness cases
pass, the module compares scalar SHA-256 and OpenCrypto for 512-, 4096- and
65536-byte blocks with a 16-byte salt. It emits three verity_crypto_cost rows.
Every measured request still checks its digest, unchanged input and output
sentinels. Require all three rows AND the final error=0 correctness summary;
partial timing output is not a successful run. Keep the VM runner deadline.

scalar_wall_us covers SHA initialization, salt/data hashing and finalization.
request_wall_us covers request allocation, completion-lock initialization,
dispatch, callback/wait, request freeing and lock destruction. The session is
reused; UIO setup and post-completion correctness checks are outside that timing.
These are aggregate elapsed times, not CPU times or latency percentiles. Provider
execution may involve scheduling, FPU setup and internal copying. The experiment
does not isolate those costs or model concurrent verifier workers. Scalar always
runs first within a case, so repeat runs and report order/cache limitations.

Reset iterations to zero for ordinary qualification. Test cryptosoft0 and each
explicit available accelerated instance separately; do not silently substitute a
provider. No speedup or production suitability is established until those runs
and the remaining error/lifetime/concurrency qualification succeed. The module
must never be loaded on the build host by any build-bsd or make target.
